HOLyHammer evaluation harness
=============================

`hhEval` evaluates HOLyHammer over whole theories: each goal of a theory
is attempted under a list of conditions (Bushy or Chainy regime; Deps,
kNN, MePo, MaSh, MeSh or per-slice premise selection; one prover alone
or the full schedule), and every result is appended to a per-theory
JSONL journal, so that an interrupted run resumes where it stopped.
`report` summarises an experiment as `summary.json` and `report.md`.

This directory is not part of the default build and is not in sigobj;
nothing in `src/holyhammer` depends on it.

Build and test, from this directory:

    ../../../bin/Holmake
    d=$(mktemp -d)
    HOL4_HAMMER_DIR=$d HOL4_SCRATCH=$d/scratch ./selftest.exe; rm -rf "$d"

The selftest launches no prover unless `HHEVAL_INTEGRATION_TEST` is set,
in which case it runs a small evaluation of `pair` and `option` with E
into the directory named by `HOL4_HAMMER_EVAL_DIR`.

The report checks of the selftest read the hand-written journals in
`test-data/hheval-report/journal`: `list.jsonl` and `arithmetic.jsonl`
hold prover cells of two conditions (E and Vampire on dependencies),
`sched.jsonl` schedule cells.

To use the harness in a HOL session:

    loadPath := Globals.HOLDIR ^ "/src/holyhammer/evaluation" :: !loadPath;
    load "hhEval";
    val condition : hhEval.condition =
      {cond_id = "bushy-deps-e", regime = hhEval.Bushy,
       selector = hhEval.Deps, engine = hhEval.Prover hhConfig.Eprover,
       timeout = 5, reconstruct = true};
    hhEval.run_eval {expname = "trial", ncore = 4, thyl = ["pair"],
                     conditions = [condition]};
    hhEval.report (hhEval.experiment_dir "trial");

An experiment lives in `hhEval.experiment_dir name`, below the
configuration key `eval.dir` (environment `HOL4_HAMMER_EVAL_DIR`), by
default `$HOLDIR/src/holyhammer/eval`.  `eval.sample` = n evaluates about
one goal in n, chosen by a hash of its name.  Each theory is evaluated by a
worker process running a script written next to the theory's sources,
which is removed when the worker ends.
